// Authored module teaching material. Names and sequence come from the catalog.
// Each explanation corresponds, in order, to one catalog lesson.
export const moduleContent = {
 'space-tech': [
  {
   context:'Design an educational Earth-observation mission that measures vegetation change. Start with the decision the data must support, not with a shopping list of satellite components.',
   concepts:[
    'A mission moves from a stakeholder need through concept studies, requirements, design, integration, verification, operations and disposal. At each review, ask whether the evidence supports the next commitment. For vegetation monitoring, define the area, revisit interval and useful data product before selecting an instrument. A successful launch alone does not demonstrate mission success.',
    'An orbit determines where a spacecraft travels, how often it can observe a location and when it can contact a ground station. For an ideal circular orbit, period depends on orbital radius, not altitude alone. Add Earth’s radius to altitude and use consistent units. This simplified calculation does not model drag, inclination, ground coverage or station visibility.',
    'Vacuum, radiation, temperature cycling and debris create different design constraints from a terrestrial classroom. Vacuum removes convective cooling; energy must move through conduction and radiation. Distinguish a hazard from a verified requirement: a radiation concern needs an environment assumption, affected component and mitigation strategy. Classroom hardware is not automatically suitable for flight.',
    'Systems engineering connects the mission objective to measurable requirements, interfaces and verification evidence. A requirement should identify what must happen and how it will be checked. “Good images” is ambiguous; a specified spatial resolution under stated conditions is testable. Track changes because a payload requirement can alter power, thermal, storage and downlink budgets.'
   ],
   example:'Educational circular-orbit estimate:\nEarth radius = 6371 km; altitude = 500 km\nOrbital radius r = 6871 km\nEarth gravitational parameter mu = 398600 km^3/s^2\nT = 2*pi*sqrt(r^3/mu) = approximately 5668 s\nPeriod = approximately 94.5 minutes\nThis is not a ground-track or communications-coverage analysis.',
   interpretation:'Using 500 km as r would be a units-and-geometry mistake. The period tells you the time for one ideal orbit; it does not tell you how often a specific farm can be imaged.',
   tasks:['Write three measurable mission requirements with a verification method.','Recalculate the ideal period at 600 km and explain the difference.','Draw a requirement-to-subsystem trace linking payload, power and downlink.'],
   evidence:'Mission brief, orbit calculation with units, and a requirements/verification matrix.',
   question:'Does a 94.5-minute orbital period guarantee a new image of the same location every 94.5 minutes?',
   answer:'No. Ground coverage also depends on orbit geometry, Earth rotation, instrument pointing and field of view. A coverage analysis is needed.'
  },
  {
   context:'Turn the mission concept into compatible subsystem budgets. The payload is intermittent, the computer runs continuously and the radio transmits only during contact.',
   concepts:[
    'Power is an instantaneous rate in watts; energy is accumulated demand in watt-hours. Estimate average demand by multiplying operating power by duty cycle, but also calculate simultaneous peak demand. Size an eclipse-energy case separately and state conversion losses and usable battery fraction. A favorable average cannot compensate for an undersized power rail.',
    'Thermal design tracks where heat is generated and how it leaves the spacecraft. Structural design considers loads, attachment points and alignment, including launch conditions. Begin with a heat-flow sketch and a load-path sketch before a detailed simulation. Record material and boundary assumptions; a simulation with incorrect constraints can give precise but misleading results.',
    'Attitude determination estimates orientation using sensors; attitude control changes orientation using actuators. A controller compares desired and estimated attitude, then commands a response. Specify pointing knowledge, pointing accuracy and stability separately. A payload can meet its resolution requirement yet fail its mission if orientation error moves the target outside the field of view.',
    'Subsystem interfaces define more than connector shape. Document voltage range, peak current, signal levels, timing, data format, units and fault behavior. Assign one owner on each side and version the agreement. Check how a radio current spike affects the computer supply instead of treating each subsystem budget as independent.'
   ],
   example:'Illustrative power budget:\nPayload: 8 W x 0.25 = 2 W average\nComputer: 2 W x 1.00 = 2 W average\nRadio: 10 W x 0.10 = 1 W average\nAverage before losses = 5 W; simultaneous peak = 20 W\n35-minute eclipse at 5 W needs 2.92 Wh at the loads\nAt 85% conversion efficiency and 80% usable capacity:\nRequired nominal energy >= 2.92/(0.85*0.80) = 4.29 Wh\nEducational estimate only; aging, temperature and margins remain to be assessed.',
   interpretation:'The 5 W average, 20 W peak and 4.29 Wh nominal-energy estimate answer different design questions. Preserve all three instead of replacing them with one “power requirement”.',
   tasks:['Create a peak/average load table and identify incompatible operating modes.','Sketch heat paths and identify a temperature-sensitive component.','Write an interface record for the payload-computer connection, including fault behavior.'],
   evidence:'Power and eclipse budgets, a thermal/load-path sketch, and an interface-control record.',
   question:'Why must a 5 W average-power design still evaluate a 20 W operating condition?',
   answer:'Components and power rails must support simultaneous peak demand. Average energy availability does not guarantee adequate instantaneous current or acceptable voltage.'
  },
  {
   context:'Create an educational telemetry chain from a simulated sensor to a ground dashboard. Every reading must remain interpretable when delayed, missing or corrupted.',
   concepts:[
    'An embedded controller has limited memory, execution time and power. Separate periodic acquisition from communication so a slow transmission does not stop sampling. Define startup behavior, watchdog recovery and safe defaults. First test with simulated readings on a desktop; only move to hardware after the timing and state transitions are understood.',
    'Telemetry is a documented message, not just a number. Include a timestamp or sequence, sensor identifier, value, units and validity status. Distinguish a real zero from a missing reading. A sequence counter helps detect gaps; a checksum detects some transmission corruption but is not authentication. Retain raw samples so processing decisions can be audited.',
    'A radio link needs a budget: transmitter power, antenna gains, path loss and other losses determine received power. Compare that result with the receiver requirement and preserve a margin. Contact duration and usable data rate determine transferable data volume. Use a simulated link in this course; real transmissions require appropriate authorization and equipment.',
    'Flight software is commonly organized around explicit operating modes and controlled transitions. A safe mode should reduce demand and preserve recovery capability. Define what happens after a sensor timeout, reset or failed command. Verification must include fault paths, not only a successful telemetry run. Prototype code is not certified flight software.'
   ],
   example:'Synthetic packet:\n{seq: 1042, time_s: 62520, sensor: "battery",\n value: 7.6, unit: "V", valid: true}\nGround receives sequences: 1040, 1041, 1043\nDetected gap: 1042 (do not invent a replacement reading)\nIllustrative contact: 120 s x 9600 bit/s = 1,152,000 bits\nAt 70% payload efficiency: 806,400 bits = 100,800 bytes\nReserve capacity for overhead, retransmissions and operations.',
   interpretation:'The useful transfer estimate is about 100.8 kB using decimal units, not the raw link-rate product. Sequence gaps belong in the dashboard as quality information.',
   tasks:['Define a telemetry schema with units and a missing-data policy.','Simulate a delayed, duplicated and missing packet and record the receiver behavior.','Draw a normal/safe/recovery state machine and test a sensor timeout.'],
   evidence:'Telemetry schema, packet-loss test log, ground display and recovery-state diagram.',
   question:'Can a checksum prove that a command came from an authorized ground operator?',
   answer:'No. A checksum checks certain accidental changes. Authentication and authorization are separate controls.'
  },
  {
   context:'Integrate the mission brief, subsystem budgets and telemetry prototype into a reviewed educational mission package. Prefer explainable behavior to unsupported autonomy claims.',
   concepts:[
    'Autonomy chooses an action without a person issuing every immediate command. Begin with a deterministic rule and compare any AI component against it. Bound the allowed actions and define a human override and safe fallback. A classifier confidence score is not evidence that a physical action is safe under unfamiliar operating conditions.',
    'Simulation makes repeatable tests possible before hardware is available, but the model has limits. Separate unit, integration and end-to-end tests. Inject faults such as missing telemetry, stale commands and low energy. Keep expected outcomes independent from the code under test so a shared mistake does not make both implementation and test appear correct.',
    'Mission operations connects planning, command approval, execution and evidence review. A command needs an identifier, intended mode, validity window and an acknowledgement policy. Document who may approve risky changes. Build a runbook covering a normal contact and a missed contact; preserve logs so an incident can be reconstructed.',
    'The capstone is a coherent mission concept plus a simulated or ground-based educational demonstrator. Link each requirement to an analysis, inspection or test. Submit interface assumptions, budgets, telemetry evidence and unresolved risks. Do not describe a classroom CubeSat/CanSat-style exercise as launch-ready hardware or imply that spectrum or launch approvals are included.'
   ],
   example:'Mission review trace:\nREQ-01: detect invalid battery telemetry\nTest: inject valid=false in the simulator\nExpected: flag unknown state; do not display a fabricated voltage\nREQ-02: recover from a missed contact\nTest: suppress one scheduled downlink\nExpected: retain queued data and follow the documented retry policy\nGate: demonstrate tests, explain limitations, then request mentor review.',
   interpretation:'Passing the simulator tests supports only the tested requirements under the declared model. It does not establish environmental qualification or operational readiness.',
   tasks:['Compare an autonomy rule with a simple baseline and define a safe fallback.','Run three fault-injection cases and link each result to a requirement.','Present a mission review package with unresolved risks and proposed next tests.'],
   evidence:'Mission demonstrator, traceable verification report, operations runbook and final review presentation.',
   question:'Does a successful simulation establish that the design will work in orbit?',
   answer:'No. Model assumptions, hardware integration, environmental qualification and operational constraints need separate evidence.'
  }
 ]
};
// Compact authoring helper; all teaching statements and labs below are explicit.
const unit=(context,concepts,example,interpretation,tasks,evidence,question,answer)=>({context,concepts,example,interpretation,tasks,evidence,question,answer});
Object.assign(moduleContent,{
 'c-programming-fundamentals':[
  unit('Build a small temperature-reporting program and learn how source becomes an executable.',[
   'The compiler translates C source and the linker resolves referenced functions. Save a .c file, compile with warnings enabled and run the resulting executable. Treat a compiler error, linker error and runtime failure as different evidence; changing random lines hides the cause.',
   'A variable has a type, a value and a lifetime. Choose an integer for a count and a floating-point type when fractions matter. Initialize before reading. Conversion can lose information: assigning 3.75 to an int does not retain its fractional part.',
   'Operators combine values, but their operand types influence the result. Integer division truncates toward zero. Parentheses make intended grouping visible; they do not change operand types. Test a calculation with values that produce a fraction rather than only conveniently divisible inputs.',
   'Formatted output requires conversion specifiers that match the arguments. Read input with a bounded buffer and validate the conversion result. Input is untrusted data, including local terminal input. A failed conversion must not leave the program using an uninitialized value.'
  ],'#include <stdio.h>\nint main(void) {\n  int count = 3;\n  double total = 71.0;\n  printf("Average: %.2f\\n", total / count);\n  return 0;\n}\n// Compile: cc -Wall -Wextra report.c -o report','The output is Average: 23.67. Replacing total with an integer changes the division before formatting, so the intermediate result must be inspected.',['Compile and explain one warning.','Compare integer and floating-point division with 7 and 2.','Add validated terminal input for the reading count.'],'Compilable source, command transcript and input validation cases.','Why does changing only the printed number of decimals not repair integer division?','Division happens before formatting; information already truncated cannot be restored by printf.'),
  unit('Turn a one-off calculation into a reusable, testable function.',[
   'A condition selects a path based on an expression. Define boundary cases before writing branches: is zero a valid count, missing data or an error? Use comparison rather than assignment in a test, and exercise both sides of every branch.',
   'A loop needs an initial state, continuation condition and progress toward termination. For n elements, valid indexes run from zero through n-1. Check empty input and one-element input first; these cases reveal incorrect loop bounds quickly.',
   'A function has an input contract, output contract and local state. Pass the data it needs instead of relying on a global variable. Local variables are not available after their lifetime ends. Return a result or explicit status that callers can handle.',
   'Debugging is hypothesis testing. Reproduce the failure with the smallest input, inspect values near the first incorrect state and change one cause at a time. A debugger breakpoint and a short assertion can reveal more than many unrelated print statements.'
  ],'double sum(const int *values, size_t n) {\n  double result = 0;\n  for (size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) result += values[i];\n  return result;\n}\n// Include <stddef.h>; caller supplies a valid array for n elements.','An empty input performs no iterations and returns zero. Computing an average requires an additional explicit n==0 policy.',['Trace the loop for n=0 and n=1.','Extract an average function with an error-status contract.','Introduce and then diagnose an off-by-one error using a local test.'],'Function contract and boundary-case test log.','What makes i <= n incorrect for a loop over n elements?','It permits index n, which lies beyond the last valid element n-1.'),
  unit('Represent a fixed set of readings without losing track of size and lifetime.',[
   'An array stores elements contiguously. Its capacity and the number of initialized values are separate facts. Pass a length to functions because an array parameter does not carry the original element count. Never infer capacity from a pointer alone.',
   'A C string is a character sequence ending in a null character. Storage must include room for that terminator. Track destination capacity when copying or reading text. A byte buffer containing characters is not automatically a valid null-terminated string.',
   'A pointer stores an address associated with a type. Dereferencing requires a valid object with a live lifetime and suitable access. A null check does not detect every invalid pointer. Do not return the address of a function-local variable.',
   'Pointer arithmetic is meaningful within an array object and its one-past position. The one-past pointer may be formed for comparison but not dereferenced. Keep indexes and lengths visible when that is clearer than arithmetic on addresses.'
  ],'int readings[] = {21, 24, 26};\nsize_t n = sizeof readings / sizeof readings[0];\nconst int *first = readings;\n// readings[1] and *(first + 1) both refer to 24.\n// first + n is one-past; do not dereference it.','The equivalence works because the pointer refers into the same live array. It is not permission to read beyond its bounds.',['Trace each valid index and address offset.','Store a short label with space for the null terminator.','Pass readings and n to a function without changing the input.'],'Array/string bounds worksheet and read-only function.','May a one-past pointer be dereferenced?','No. It can support boundary comparison but does not designate an array element.'),
  unit('Assemble a terminal record manager with explicit ownership and reliable file handling.',[
   'A structure groups related fields into a value; an enum names a finite set of states. Keep a record ID separate from its array position so deletion or sorting does not silently change identity. Validate a state before using it in a decision.',
   'Opening, reading, writing and closing a file can fail. Check return values and distinguish end-of-file from an error. Define a record format and reject malformed rows. Do not assume a write succeeded simply because the output file exists.',
   'Review every buffer length, object lifetime and allocation owner. Pair successful allocation with one appropriate release and avoid using a pointer after release. Prefer a fixed-capacity design for an introductory project when dynamic allocation adds no useful learning value.',
   'The capstone joins input validation, functions, records and persistence. Build add/list/search behavior first, then test malformed input and a missing file. A README should explain compilation, record format, limits and recovery behavior rather than only show a successful screenshot.'
  ],'Record format: id,title,status\nExample: 101,Review pointers,OPEN\nLoad -> validate fields -> reject duplicates -> store record\nUpdate -> validate ID -> change value -> check write result\nTests: empty file; malformed row; duplicate ID; unavailable path.','A malformed row should produce a clear diagnostic without corrupting records that were already validated.',['Define the record schema and capacity.','Implement add, list and find with invalid-input handling.','Demonstrate save/reload and document error behavior.'],'Terminal application, reproducible tests and project README.','Why should a record ID not simply be its current array index?','Indexes change after reordering or deletion; a stable ID preserves record identity.')
 ],
 'cpp-essentials':[
  unit('Create a small modern C++ task utility with clear value semantics.',[
   'Compile a minimal program and distinguish source, object files and the linked executable. Enable warnings and select a documented language standard. Keep the build command in the README so a second learner can reproduce the result.',
   'Use types to express what a value means. A count, an optional result and text are different concepts. Prefer initialization and explicit control flow. Avoid narrowing conversions that silently discard information when data moves between types.',
   'Pass small values by value and inspect larger objects through const references when appropriate. A reference does not extend every lifetime. State whether a function mutates its argument, and never return a reference to a destroyed local object.',
   'Headers declare interfaces that other translation units consume; source files provide implementations. Use namespaces to organize names rather than importing every name globally in a header. Include what you use and prevent repeated header definitions.'
  ],'#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\nvoid greet(const std::string& name) {\n  std::cout << "Hello, " << name << "\\n";\n}\nint main() { greet("Learner"); }','The function reads the string without mutating it. Its reference is valid for the duration of the call.',['Compile with warnings enabled.','Separate greet into a header and implementation.','Explain why returning a reference to a local string is invalid.'],'Reproducible two-file project and lifetime explanation.','Does const on a reference make the referenced object live forever?','No. It restricts mutation through that reference, not object lifetime.'),
  unit('Model task records with invariants instead of a collection of unrelated fields.',[
   'A class combines state with operations that preserve its rules. Define which states are valid before choosing public methods. Keep fields private when callers could otherwise create invalid combinations. Use a plain value type when no invariant needs protection.',
   'A constructor establishes a usable object. Initialize members directly and validate required data at the boundary. Avoid creating an object that callers must remember to initialize later. Describe how invalid constructor input is reported.',
   'Inheritance models a substitutable relationship, not simply code reuse. Ask whether a derived object can safely satisfy every expectation of the base. Composition is often clearer for a task that has a reminder rather than is a reminder.',
   'Runtime polymorphism lets a caller use a stable interface while implementations vary. A base used for polymorphic deletion needs an appropriate virtual destructor. Keep the interface small and test implementations against the same behavioral contract.'
  ],'struct Notifier {\n  virtual ~Notifier() = default;\n  virtual void send(const std::string& message) = 0;\n};\n// ConsoleNotifier implements send without changing its contract.','The caller depends on the notification contract, not console details. The interface does not need to expose storage or UI concerns.',['Define one invariant for Task.','Initialize a Task through its constructor.','Compare composition and inheritance for a reminder feature.'],'Class diagram, invariant tests and interface example.','Is inheritance automatically the best way to reuse a method?','No. Prefer a design that preserves substitutability; composition often provides reuse without an unsuitable hierarchy.'),
  unit('Store tasks and query them with standard containers and algorithms.',[
   'A vector owns a sequence; a map associates keys with values. Choose based on lookup, ordering and update needs. Inserting into a vector may reallocate storage, invalidating references into its former storage. Keep stable IDs instead of long-lived element addresses.',
   'An iterator describes a position in a range. The end iterator is a boundary, not an element to read. Container modification can invalidate iterators. State which operations occur while iterating and avoid changing the container without understanding those rules.',
   'Standard algorithms separate traversal from the operation being applied. A predicate should express one test without surprising side effects. Use a const range when reading only. Compare a clear algorithm call with a manual loop using the same edge cases.',
   'Exceptions report failures that normal return flow cannot handle locally. Catch where recovery or useful context is possible. Do not silently swallow a failure and pretend the operation succeeded. Resource-owning objects should clean up during stack unwinding.'
  ],'std::vector<int> scores{2, 4, 6};\nauto found = std::find(scores.begin(), scores.end(), 4);\nif (found != scores.end()) std::cout << *found;\n// Headers: <vector>, <algorithm>, <iostream>','The end check is required before dereferencing. Searching an empty vector is valid and returns end.',['Search an empty and non-empty vector.','Replace a saved reference with lookup by ID.','Handle a failed input conversion without losing prior tasks.'],'Container tests and documented error handling.','What does find return when the target is absent?','The supplied end iterator, which must not be dereferenced.'),
  unit('Make resource lifetime predictable and ship a complete console application.',[
   'Automatic local objects are destroyed when their scope ends. Dynamic storage can outlive a scope but needs an owner. Storage location alone does not define a good design; the important question is which object is responsible for cleanup.',
   'A unique_ptr expresses exclusive ownership; shared_ptr represents shared ownership with reference counting. Prefer exclusive ownership unless the problem requires sharing. A raw pointer may observe an object without owning it, but the observer must not outlive its target.',
   'RAII couples resource acquisition with object lifetime. Files, locks and memory can all use this pattern. When an owning object is destroyed, its cleanup runs even when execution leaves through an exception. Avoid duplicating manual cleanup on every return path.',
   'Complete the task utility using values and standard containers. Add load/save, predictable errors and tests for empty input and missing IDs. Explain ownership in the README and demonstrate that cancellation or failure does not leak a resource.'
  ],'{\n  std::ifstream input("tasks.txt");\n  if (!input) { /* report failure */ }\n  // Read validated records.\n} // input closes its file through object destruction.','The file stream owns its resource. Cleanup follows scope exit rather than relying on a separate close call along every branch.',['Draw the ownership relationships.','Load and save a task list using stream objects.','Test a missing file and malformed record.'],'Console capstone, ownership map and regression cases.','Why is RAII useful when a function has several exit paths?','Cleanup follows the owning object’s lifetime, reducing duplicated and easily missed release logic.')
 ],
 'javascript-foundations':[
  unit('Build predictable transformations before connecting a user interface.',[
   'Use const when a binding will not be reassigned and let when reassignment is necessary. An object held by const can still be mutated. Distinguish null, undefined and an empty string so missing data does not accidentally look like a valid answer.',
   'A function should make its inputs and returned result clear. Separate calculation from DOM updates to make testing easier. Avoid reading hidden global state for a simple filter. Repeating the same input should produce the same result when no side effects are required.',
   'Arrays represent ordered collections; objects represent named properties. filter creates a new selected array, while some operations mutate the original. Preserve source data so clearing a search can restore the original list. Use stable identifiers for records.',
   'Read the error message and stack trace before changing code. Reproduce the smallest failure and inspect the value at the boundary where its type or shape differs from expectations. Handle expected failures explicitly instead of hiding every error in an empty catch.'
  ],'const courses = ["C", "C++", "JavaScript"];\nconst query = "script";\nconst result = courses.filter(name =>\n  name.toLowerCase().includes(query.toLowerCase()));\n// result: ["JavaScript"]; courses remains unchanged.','A search result is a view of the original collection. A query matching nothing should not delete course records.',['Test empty, mixed-case and unmatched queries.','Extract a reusable filter function.','Explain one mutation that would break reset behavior.'],'Pure filtering function and input/output tests.','Does const prevent changing the contents of an array?','No. It prevents rebinding that variable; it does not freeze the array.'),
  unit('Connect a course filter to an accessible browser interface.',[
   'Select the specific DOM element needed and handle a missing match. Keep data separate from rendered nodes. Use textContent for user-derived text so input is not interpreted as markup. A DOM selector is a lookup, not a guarantee that the element exists.',
   'An event listener responds to an interaction after the page has loaded. Use the event target deliberately and avoid installing duplicate listeners on every render. Test both typing and keyboard activation, not only mouse clicks.',
   'Browser validation improves feedback but does not secure a server. Label every field, explain errors near the relevant input and preserve entered data after an error. Validate again at the trusted boundary when a backend exists.',
   'Loading, empty, error and success states need meaningful text, not only a color change. Keep focus visible and do not remove the control currently being used without managing focus. Announce important dynamic status through an appropriate live region.'
  ],'search.addEventListener("input", event => {\n  const q = event.target.value.trim().toLowerCase();\n  const visible = courses.filter(x => x.toLowerCase().includes(q));\n  output.textContent = visible.length ? visible.join(", ") : "No matches";\n});','The listener updates a text result on each input event. It does not interpret the query as HTML or mutate the source list.',['Wire a labeled search field.','Add a result count and no-results message.','Test keyboard navigation and resetting the filter.'],'Accessible filtering page and interaction checklist.','Is client-side form validation a replacement for server validation?','No. Clients can be bypassed or modified; the trusted boundary must validate independently.'),
  unit('Represent asynchronous work without misleading loading or error states.',[
   'A promise represents an eventual result or rejection. Creating a promise does not make every operation inside it non-blocking. Return chained work so callers can await it, and attach error handling where a failure can be explained or recovered.',
   'await pauses the current async function, not the entire browser. Independent operations may run concurrently, while dependent operations must preserve order. Use try/finally to restore UI state after either success or failure.',
   'fetch can resolve even when an HTTP response indicates an error. Inspect response.ok before trusting a JSON body, and validate the expected data shape. A parsed JSON value is not automatically a correctly typed course record.',
   'Rapid requests can finish out of order. Cancel stale work or compare a request identifier before updating the screen. Keep existing results or a clear loading message while work is pending, and provide a useful retry path after failure.'
  ],'async function loadCourses() {\n  const response = await fetch("./courses.json");\n  if (!response.ok) throw new Error("Course request failed");\n  const data = await response.json();\n  if (!Array.isArray(data)) throw new Error("Expected a list");\n  return data;\n}','HTTP success and data-shape validation are separate checks. Neither a network response nor JSON parsing proves that the UI can use the value.',['Simulate loading, failure and an empty response.','Add a retry button.','Prevent an older response from replacing a newer search result.'],'Asynchronous page with explicit state transitions.','Does fetch reject automatically for every HTTP error status?','No. Inspect the response status or ok flag before processing the body.'),
  unit('Package the browser project so another developer can run and test it.',[
   'ES modules declare explicit imports and exports. Keep filtering logic independent from DOM wiring so it can be reused and tested. Avoid circular dependencies that make initialization order difficult to reason about.',
   'A development server provides a consistent origin and module loading. Record setup commands and dependency versions. A production build may differ from the development server; test the built files rather than assuming development success proves the release works.',
   'Tests compare an expected behavior with an observed result. Cover normal, empty and invalid inputs. Add an interaction test for the complete user flow, since a correct helper function can still be wired to the wrong field or button.',
   'Deliver the filter application with clear states, accessible controls and reproducible setup. Keep secrets out of browser code. Include a small test fixture and explain what is simulated, particularly when network or authentication services are not connected.'
  ],'project/\n  index.html\n  src/filter.js\n  src/ui.js\n  data/courses.json\n  tests/filter.test.js\n  README.md\nContract: filterCourses(items, query) returns a new array.','The file boundary mirrors responsibilities: data transformation, interface behavior, fixtures and verification each have a clear home.',['Export the filter helper.','Add three boundary tests and one browser journey.','Document setup, limits and deployment steps.'],'Interactive app, tests and reproducible README.','Why test the built site as well as individual functions?','Asset paths, event wiring and deployment behavior can fail even when isolated functions are correct.')
 ]
});
Object.assign(moduleContent,{
 'entrepreneurship':[
  unit('Use a fictional crop-advisory business to learn venture design. You may also use your own case; taking the course does not submit that idea to the academy.',[
   'Frame an opportunity as a change in a specific customer situation, not a list of product features. Identify the triggering event, current workaround and consequence. An interesting technology is not evidence of demand.',
   'A problem statement names who experiences the problem, what happens and why it matters. Separate observations from assumptions. Avoid combining several unrelated audiences into one statement because their urgency and purchasing behavior may differ.',
   'Choose a narrow initial segment that can be reached and studied. Distinguish the user, buyer and decision-maker. A farmer using advice and an organization paying for it may need different evidence and different value propositions.',
   'In the course, review a problem brief against clarity, evidence and uncertainty. This is an assignment review, not venture admission. The separate Submit an idea journey requests evaluation for mentor-supported venture progression; it has its own decision and status.'
  ],'Case hypothesis:\nSegment: growers managing small irrigated vegetable plots\nSituation: deciding when to irrigate after irregular rainfall\nCurrent workaround: personal observation and calls to peers\nUnknown: frequency, cost of mistakes and willingness to change\nDo not replace these unknowns with invented market evidence.','A precise hypothesis tells you whom to interview and what behavior to investigate. It is still a hypothesis until evidence is collected.',['Write a segment-specific problem statement.','Label every claim as observed, inferred or untested.','Review a peer brief without promising admission or funding.'],'Problem brief and assumption register.','Does completing this module submit a venture application?','No. Course learning and venture application are separate; submission requires an explicit action in the idea-intake journey.'),
  unit('Plan discovery interviews that can disprove your hypothesis as well as support it.',[
   'Prepare open questions about recent behavior and ask for a concrete example. Seek consent before recording. Decide how notes will be stored and anonymized. A script should guide inquiry, not pressure an interviewee into endorsing the idea.',
   'Ask what happened the last time the problem occurred, what the person tried and what it cost in time or effort. Avoid pitching your solution first. Hypothetical enthusiasm is weaker evidence than actual behavior and current workarounds.',
   'Synthesize notes by segment, situation and recurring pattern. Preserve contradictory observations instead of deleting them as outliers. Distinguish an interview quote from your interpretation and record what additional evidence would change your conclusion.',
   'Review whether the evidence supports continuing, narrowing or revising the problem. The course exercise uses a documented decision with uncertainty. An actual venture validation gate is a separate review of a submitted application and artifacts, not an automatic course-completion event.'
  ],'Interview prompt: “Tell me about the last irrigation decision you found difficult.”\nFollow-up: “What did you do next?”\nEvidence table: situation | workaround | consequence | quote | interpretation\nDecision: narrow the segment if problems differ materially between groups.','The question elicits a past event rather than an optimistic forecast about a product that does not exist.',['Write five behavioral interview questions.','Analyze a consented or synthetic set of interview notes.','Record a continue/revise/stop decision with counterevidence.'],'Interview guide, evidence synthesis and validation memo.','Why is “Would you use my app?” insufficient by itself?','It measures hypothetical interest, not demonstrated urgency, behavior or willingness to change.'),
  unit('Translate a validated problem into a testable business model.',[
   'A value proposition connects a customer job to a specific improvement over the current alternative. State the benefit in the customer’s terms. A feature such as an AI dashboard matters only if it changes a useful decision or outcome.',
   'Separate how value is delivered from how revenue is collected. A subscription, usage charge or service fee changes incentives and cash timing. Treat a proposed price as a hypothesis and test it ethically rather than presenting it as established willingness to pay.',
   'Unit economics estimates contribution at a meaningful unit such as a customer or transaction. Keep variable costs, fixed costs and acquisition cost distinct. Explain the period and assumptions; an attractive gross margin does not guarantee positive cash flow.',
   'A review tests whether the customer, channel, revenue and cost assumptions fit together. Identify the assumption most likely to invalidate the model. Make a revision based on evidence instead of adding unsupported market-size or revenue claims.'
  ],'Illustrative monthly subscription: 500 currency units\nVariable service cost/customer: 180\nContribution/customer/month: 320\nFixed monthly cost: 32,000\nSimplified break-even: 100 paying customers\nExcludes acquisition cost, churn, taxes and cash-timing effects.','The arithmetic is a teaching model, not a forecast. Changing retention or service effort can materially change viability.',['Write one value proposition for a narrow segment.','Build a revenue/cost assumption table.','Stress-test contribution when variable cost rises by 25%.'],'Business-model canvas and assumption-based unit-economics worksheet.','Does positive contribution per customer prove the business is profitable?','No. Fixed costs, acquisition, retention, cash timing and other obligations still matter.'),
  unit('Build the smallest demonstrator that tests the largest remaining uncertainty.',[
   'An MVP is a bounded learning experiment, not a smaller copy of every planned feature. Write the question it must answer and remove features that do not help answer it. A manual service or clickable prototype may be sufficient.',
   'Choose prototype fidelity based on the risk being tested. A clickable flow tests comprehension, while a working integration tests technical feasibility. Document what is simulated so feedback is not based on capabilities the prototype does not have.',
   'A test plan identifies participant criteria, task, observation method and decision threshold before the test. Include failure and abandonment, not just completion. Separate usability findings from evidence of willingness to pay.',
   'Review scope, privacy, reliability and test readiness before exposing the prototype to participants. Course review checks the assignment evidence. A venture program’s MVP gate is a distinct decision with its own reviewer and artifact history.'
  ],'Learning question: can growers interpret an irrigation recommendation?\nPrototype: three screens with synthetic weather data\nTask: explain the recommendation and choose a next action\nObserve: confusion, incorrect assumptions, confidence and abandonment\nBoundary: no live agricultural advice is delivered.','A prototype can test comprehension without pretending to supply reliable real-world recommendations.',['Define the MVP learning question and exclusions.','Produce a clickable flow or documented manual service.','Write a test plan with observable success and failure criteria.'],'Prototype, architecture sketch and test plan.','Must an MVP automate every step of the proposed service?','No. It must test the chosen uncertainty credibly; manual steps can be appropriate when disclosed.'),
  unit('Plan a limited pilot with a clear audience, support boundary and rollback decision.',[
   'A pilot has a limited scope, duration, participant set and purpose. Explain what participants will receive and what remains experimental. Set a stop condition for harmful or misleading behavior rather than waiting for a final presentation.',
   'A go-to-market experiment tests one channel and one message with a defined audience. Track the full path from exposure to qualified interest. Clicks alone do not demonstrate a useful acquisition channel.',
   'A launch checklist covers access, support, privacy, incident handling, ownership and data recovery. Assign a responsible person to each item. An unchecked critical item should change the launch decision, not disappear into a completion percentage.',
   'A launch gate compares evidence with pre-agreed readiness criteria. Record approve, revise or stop and the reasons. In the course, this is a simulated review; publishing a lesson or completing a quiz does not authorize a real venture launch.'
  ],'Pilot charter: 10 consenting participants; 2 weeks; synthetic recommendations\nMeasure: task completion and misunderstanding reports\nSupport owner: named team member\nStop: participant treats simulated output as operational advice\nRollback: disable recommendation screen; preserve consented research notes.','The stop condition is part of the pilot design, not evidence that the team expects the experiment to fail.',['Write a bounded pilot charter.','Design one channel/message experiment.','Complete a launch checklist with owners and rollback steps.'],'Pilot plan, acquisition experiment and launch-readiness review.','Why is a stop condition needed before a pilot starts?','It enables a timely, accountable response when risk exceeds the agreed boundary.'),
  unit('Use evidence to decide what to improve, continue or stop.',[
   'Choose a metric that represents the behavior the venture needs, not merely activity. State its numerator, denominator, time window and exclusions. Distinguish acquisition, activation, retention and revenue rather than combining them into an ambiguous growth score.',
   'A feedback loop connects observation to a prioritized change and a follow-up measurement. Tag feedback by segment and context. Do not let one loud request silently become the product roadmap without checking whether it represents the intended audience.',
   'Report experiment results against the original question and acknowledge sample limitations. Separate observed differences from causal claims. If several variables changed together, explain why the result cannot identify which change caused the effect.',
   'A traction review combines quantitative behavior, customer evidence and operating costs. Record what improved and what did not. A mentor’s positive review is not proof of product-market fit or a guarantee of investment.'
  ],'Week 1: 40 signups; 20 complete the key task\nWeek 2: 30 signups; 21 complete the key task\nActivation: 50% then 70%; counts and rates tell different stories\nCheck cohort comparability before attributing the change to a new design.','The activation rate rose while signup count fell. A single headline metric would hide part of the decision.',['Define three metrics with denominators.','Turn feedback into one testable improvement.','Write an experiment report with limitations and next decision.'],'Metric dictionary, feedback log and traction review.','Can a before/after comparison alone prove a design caused an improvement?','No. Audience, timing and other changes may explain the difference.'),
  unit('Prepare a responsible operating plan and present the evidence behind the venture.',[
   'An operating model assigns decisions, delivery work, support and escalation. Identify bottlenecks that depend on one founder. Document repeatable work before hiring or automating so the team knows what capability it is trying to scale.',
   'Identify sensitive data, access boundaries and obligations that require qualified advice. Build an issue register with owners and due dates. This lesson is a readiness exercise, not legal advice or a declaration that the venture is compliant.',
   'A scale plan connects demand assumptions with capacity, cost and quality. Model a plausible increase and identify which limit is reached first. Define checkpoints and rollback options instead of treating growth as an irreversible all-at-once launch.',
   'The final showcase presents the problem, evidence, model, prototype, pilot results and remaining risks. Separate facts from forecasts. Course certification reflects the stated learning requirements; investor introductions, venture selection and funding decisions remain independent.'
  ],'Scale scenario: 100 -> 500 active customers\nCheck: support hours, service cost, data permissions and failure recovery\nConstraint: one reviewer can assess 40 cases/day\nDecision: improve workflow and test capacity before widening acquisition\nShowcase appendix: evidence links and unresolved assumptions.','A scale plan is credible when it identifies constraints, not when it assumes every cost and process scales linearly.',['Map operating roles and escalation.','Build a capacity scenario and risk register.','Present a final evidence portfolio with clearly labeled forecasts.'],'Operating plan, scale scenario and final course showcase.','Does course completion imply venture acceptance or funding?','No. Those are separate applications and decisions; the course teaches the skills and evaluates learning evidence.')
 ]
});
Object.assign(moduleContent,{
 'ai-engineering':[
  unit('Prepare a dependable service boundary before adding a model.',[
   'An API contract specifies inputs, outputs, errors and permissions. Validate requests at the service boundary and return predictable error shapes. Keep a model call behind this boundary so clients do not depend on one provider’s response format.',
   'Model transactional records separately from retrieved documents and generated text. Identify primary keys, ownership and retention. A vector index supports similarity lookup; it does not replace the transactional database or enforce every access rule automatically.',
   'Use version control to make changes reviewable and tests to preserve contracts. Include invalid-input and permission-denial cases. A successful prompt example is not a regression suite; keep representative fixtures and expected behaviors under review.',
   'A deployed service needs configuration, secrets, logs, health checks and a recovery plan. Separate environment configuration from source code. Define what happens when a dependency times out, rather than letting an infrastructure failure appear as a confident answer.'
  ],'POST /assistant/query\nInput: {question, conversationId}\nIdentity: authenticated server context\nOutput: {status, answer, sources, requestId}\nFailures: invalid request; access denied; source unavailable\nNever accept a client-supplied role as authorization.','The contract lets the UI handle success and failure without guessing from a generated paragraph.',['Define request/response schemas.','Create a synthetic database fixture with two organizations.','Test invalid input and dependency timeout.'],'Service contract, fixture and boundary tests.','Should the browser decide the user’s trusted role?','No. The server derives authorization from trusted identity and policy.'),
  unit('Build a grounded answer pipeline for approved knowledge sources.',[
   'An LLM predicts output from context; fluent language does not prove factual accuracy. Define the task, acceptable evidence and abstention behavior. Evaluate answerable and unanswerable cases instead of assuming a larger model removes the need for verification.',
   'Separate task instructions from retrieved evidence and user input. Context has a finite budget, so select relevant material and preserve source identifiers. Treat instructions inside documents as untrusted data rather than authority to change system behavior.',
   'Structured output makes parsing predictable, but schema validity does not establish semantic correctness. Validate values and permission before executing a tool. A proposed action should remain a proposal until the authorized workflow accepts it.',
   'Embeddings support similarity search over representations of content. Retrieval quality depends on chunking, metadata and evaluation. Filter evidence by the requesting user’s permissions before it reaches the model, and retain citations that actually support the answer.'
  ],'Question -> identity-aware retrieval -> evidence selection\nEvidence: [{sourceId:"policy-4", text:"..."}]\nAnswer: {status:"supported", text:"...", sources:["policy-4"]}\nNo sufficient evidence -> status:"needs_review"\nProposed write -> validate -> authorize -> approve -> execute','A source list is useful only when the cited evidence supports the claim and the user may access it.',['Create answerable and unanswerable fixtures.','Compare two chunking choices on the same questions.','Reject a schema-valid but unauthorized tool request.'],'Grounded response set and retrieval evaluation.','Does valid JSON prove a generated action is safe?','No. Meaning, permissions and workflow approval require separate validation.'),
  unit('Choose bounded orchestration for tasks that combine tools and modalities.',[
   'An agent selects steps within an allowed action space. Give it a stopping condition, budget and escalation route. Persist only memory that serves a defined purpose and respect retention and permissions; unlimited conversation history is not a memory strategy.',
   'Tool protocols and reusable skills can standardize integration, but they do not grant trust. Validate tool descriptions, inputs and outputs. Use multiple agents only where independent responsibilities justify coordination cost and potential disagreement.',
   'Voice adds turn-taking, interruption and latency concerns to a text workflow. Separate speech recognition, task reasoning and speech synthesis. Provide confirmation for consequential actions and a way to recover when recognition changes a name or number.',
   'Document and image workflows need provenance and uncertainty handling. OCR may misread tables or lose reading order. Preserve page references and route low-confidence or consequential fields to review instead of treating extracted text as verified ground truth.'
  ],'Voice request -> transcript -> clarify ambiguous record ID\nRead-only lookup -> summarize with source reference\nProposed change -> explicit confirmation -> authorized write\nTimeout or interruption -> preserve safe state; do not retry a write blindly.','A conversational interface still needs the same authorization and idempotency boundaries as a conventional application.',['Draw task states and stop conditions.','Test an interrupted voice-action scenario with synthetic transcripts.','Compare extracted fields with their source page.'],'Orchestration diagram and multimodal failure cases.','Does a standardized tool connection mean its output is trusted?','No. A protocol standardizes exchange; content and authority still require validation.'),
  unit('Measure and operate an AI service under realistic failure conditions.',[
   'An evaluation set needs representative tasks, expected evidence and clear scoring criteria. Include adversarial, restricted and insufficient-context cases. Guardrails reduce specific risks but do not make all outputs correct; track their false positives and missed cases.',
   'Package a reproducible service and gate releases with tests. Separate build artifacts from runtime secrets. A rollback must restore compatible behavior and data assumptions, not simply redeploy a previous image without checking migrations.',
   'Observe latency, retrieval outcomes, model usage and tool decisions with request identifiers. Avoid logging unnecessary private content. A trace should help locate a failure without becoming a second uncontrolled store of sensitive data.',
   'Bound concurrency, token usage and retries. Estimate cost using measured request patterns and test what happens at a dependency limit. Use explicit authorization and tenant isolation; a cost optimization must not reuse private cached content across users.'
  ],'Release gate:\n- permission-denial tests pass\n- grounded-answer rubric reviewed\n- timeout produces a recoverable error\n- repeated write request has one effect\n- logs contain request IDs, not unrestricted document bodies','Operational quality combines task behavior, security boundaries and recovery, not a single model benchmark.',['Define an evaluation rubric.','Simulate a provider outage and verify fallback behavior.','Produce a release and rollback checklist.'],'Evaluation report, operational dashboard plan and runbook.','Should retrying a failed request always repeat its side effects?','No. State-changing operations need idempotency and a deliberate retry policy.'),
  unit('Connect enterprise systems without bypassing identity or audit boundaries.',[
   'Authentication identifies the caller; authorization determines allowed actions. OAuth and OIDC have different roles in delegated access and identity. Validate the intended audience and permissions through trusted libraries and configuration rather than interpreting token text as sufficient proof.',
   'An adapter should expose a small, validated contract over the enterprise API or database. Handle pagination, rate limits and schema changes. Use read-only credentials for read-only work and avoid making unrestricted database access available to a model.',
   'SharePoint, SAP and CRM are examples of enterprise information sources, not separate academy consulting offerings. Map document or record permissions into retrieval. Preserve source identifiers and update rules so an answer does not rely indefinitely on stale enterprise data.',
   'Legacy and service-management systems may lack modern event guarantees. Use explicit mappings, reconciliation and auditable retry handling. A connector must distinguish “request sent” from “change confirmed”, especially after a timeout.'
  ],'Adapter request: {recordId, operation:"read"}\nTrusted identity -> policy check -> source API\nResponse: {sourceId, version, permittedFields, fetchedAt}\nWrite path: validated command + idempotency key + receipt\nReconcile an uncertain timeout before replaying a mutation.','The adapter narrows capability and preserves evidence. It is not a shortcut around source-system permissions.',['Design a read-only CRM fixture adapter.','Map two users to different source permissions.','Demonstrate a timeout with reconciliation rather than duplicate writes.'],'Adapter contract, access matrix and audit trace.','Does access to an integration account justify exposing every source record?','No. The requesting user’s allowed scope must still govern returned evidence.'),
  unit('Select a solution based on the customer’s workflow and evidence needs.',[
   'Use retrieval when answers need current external evidence; consider adaptation when a measured behavior needs improvement. A smaller model may fit a bounded task, but capacity, latency and maintenance trade-offs need testing. These choices can be combined rather than treated as slogans.',
   'A deterministic workflow is preferable when steps and decisions are known. An agent may help when the path genuinely varies, but it adds evaluation and control needs. Start with the simplest design that meets the task and add autonomy only for a demonstrated benefit.',
   'Observe the current workflow, identify users and establish a baseline before estimating value. Include exception handling and approval work in the cost picture. A convincing demonstration is not evidence that the solution fits daily operations.',
   'The capstone combines discovery, architecture, an adapter, grounded behavior, evaluation and an operations plan. Show denied requests and failures alongside successes. Present unresolved assumptions and the next validation step instead of claiming production readiness from a prototype.'
  ],'Decision record:\nNeed: current policy answers with citations\nInitial choice: permission-filtered retrieval + deterministic response workflow\nWhy not autonomous writes: no validated approval process yet\nMeasure: evidence quality, task completion and escalation rate\nNext gate: supervised pilot with bounded users','The design follows a task and control boundary, rather than selecting a technology before understanding the problem.',['Compare two architectures against explicit criteria.','Write a customer discovery and baseline memo.','Demonstrate a capstone with evaluation and operational evidence.'],'Architecture decision record and forward-deployment portfolio.','When should a deterministic workflow remain the preferred design?','When known steps meet the need reliably and additional autonomy provides no demonstrated benefit.')
 ],
 'ai-leadership':[
  unit('Evaluate AI proposals by capability, evidence and consequences of error.',[
   'Fluent output can conceal missing evidence. Ask what a proposed system does well, where it fails and who reviews consequential errors. A demonstration should include difficult and unanswerable cases, not only a polished success path.',
   'Retrieval supplies external context; fine-tuning changes learned behavior; a smaller model may suit a bounded task. Ask which problem each choice solves and what evidence supports it. None removes the need for permissions, evaluation and an operating owner.',
   'Autonomy changes who selects the next step and therefore changes risk. Map which actions are reversible, which require approval and where the system must stop. Do not equate an agent demonstration with permission to automate the whole business process.',
   'Voice, image and document workflows can expand access but also introduce recognition errors and privacy concerns. Evaluate the entire user journey, including confirmation and correction. Ask whether a simpler interface would solve the same problem with fewer failure modes.'
  ],'Proposal review: “AI resolves all service tickets.”\nClarify: which ticket classes, source evidence, permitted actions and escalation?\nRequire: examples of unsupported questions and a denied change\nDecision: bounded assisted workflow before broader autonomy','The review converts a broad claim into testable scope and accountable decisions.',['Classify three proposed capabilities.','List failure consequences and human controls.','Rewrite an unsupported automation claim as a bounded pilot.'],'Capability assessment and decision-boundary map.','Does a fluent demonstration establish operational reliability?','No. Representative evaluation and controls are needed beyond the demonstration.'),
  unit('Choose an opportunity that the organization can responsibly validate.',[
   'Start with an observed bottleneck and a named user group. Record the present process, exceptions and baseline. An opportunity is stronger when an owner can explain the decision it will improve, not merely the technology they want to purchase.',
   'Data readiness includes quality, access rights, provenance and ongoing maintenance. A large document collection is not automatically usable. Establish who owns permission changes and how stale or disputed information is handled.',
   'Compare build, buy and partner options against integration, control, skills, cost and exit needs. Identify dependencies and switching costs. A vendor feature list does not prove that the organization can operate the resulting workflow.',
   'Estimate value from a measured baseline and explicit assumptions. Include review time, integration, support and error costs, not only model usage. Report a range and sensitivity analysis; teaching examples are not financial forecasts or investment advice.'
  ],'Candidate scorecard: value | feasibility | data readiness | consequence of error\nPilot cost: integration + user training + review + operation\nBenefit hypothesis: reduced handling time without increased consequential errors\nDecision owner: business lead with risk and technical input','A scorecard supports discussion; the weights and assumptions must remain visible rather than creating false precision.',['Compare three candidate workflows.','Write a data/permission readiness checklist.','Stress-test one value assumption.'],'Opportunity portfolio and assumption-based business case.','Why include human review time in AI economics?','It is part of the actual operating workflow and can materially change the value case.'),
  unit('Assign responsibility for safe operation and meaningful adoption.',[
   'Identify the consequences of error and place human control where it can prevent harm. A reviewer needs time, evidence and authority, not just a button to approve. Define stop conditions before the pilot begins.',
   'Auditability requires knowing who requested, approved and executed an action. Limit access and retain only necessary evidence. Confirm that logs do not expose sensitive content more broadly than the underlying system.',
   'Assign business ownership, technical operation, incident response and change approval. Define escalation paths and handover responsibilities. An AI project without an operating owner can succeed in a demo and fail immediately after launch.',
   'Adoption requires role-specific training and feedback channels. Explain what the system cannot do and how to challenge an answer. Track useful task completion, not only logins, and avoid treating reluctance as a problem to solve by removing human oversight.'
  ],'Control record: action | risk | approver | evidence | stop condition\nExample: invoice recommendation -> human approval before payment\nIncident owner: operations lead\nChange owner: product lead with risk review\nTraining: recognizing unsupported or uncertain output','The operating model makes responsibility visible when an exception occurs, not only during normal use.',['Build a responsibility matrix.','Define a review and escalation workflow.','Plan training for users and reviewers.'],'Governance charter, access matrix and adoption plan.','Is a nominal human approval step sufficient by itself?','No. The reviewer must have appropriate evidence, authority and capacity to review meaningfully.'),
  unit('Present a 90-day plan with a decision gate rather than a guaranteed outcome.',[
   'Select a small set of use cases that share a measurable goal and a ready owner. Sequence dependencies deliberately. Do not launch every candidate simply because the technology is available or competitors are discussing it.',
   'Define the pilot’s audience, baseline, measures and guardrails before launch. Pair efficiency measures with quality and risk measures. Decide how results will be collected so the final review does not depend on anecdotes.',
   'Schedule checkpoints for data access, evaluation, user readiness and incident handling. Each gate needs a decision owner and evidence. A checkpoint should be able to stop or revise the work, not merely confirm that a meeting happened.',
   'Present scope, owners, cost assumptions, delivery phases and stop/scale criteria. Distinguish committed work from hypotheses. The plan should explain what leadership will decide at day 90 and what evidence would justify a different direction.'
  ],'Days 1-30: baseline, data permissions, task evaluation\nDays 31-60: bounded pilot and trained reviewers\nDays 61-90: measure quality, cost and adoption; review incidents\nFinal decision: stop, revise, or controlled expansion\nNo automatic scale decision based on elapsed time.','A 90-day schedule organizes evidence collection; it is not a promise that transformation will be complete in three months.',['Choose and justify a pilot.','Set paired value and quality measures.','Present a phased plan with decision gates.'],'90-day execution blueprint and executive review deck.','What if the pilot improves speed but violates an agreed quality guardrail?','Stop or revise according to the guardrail; speed alone does not justify expansion.')
 ]
});
Object.assign(moduleContent,{
 'data-structures-algorithms':[
  unit('Compare algorithms using input size, operation counts and explicit assumptions.',[
   'Big O describes how a resource bound grows with input size; it is not an exact stopwatch prediction. Count a relevant operation, vary n and state the case being analyzed. A constant factor can matter in practice without changing the growth class.',
   'An array supports indexed access because elements occupy a predictable contiguous layout. Inserting in the middle may require shifting later elements. Draw indexes before changing values, and distinguish capacity from the number of active elements.',
   'Two pointers and sliding windows exploit structure to avoid repeating work. State the invariant that remains true as a pointer moves. Some windows rely on nonnegative values or another monotonic property; do not apply the pattern without checking its assumptions.',
   'Binary search needs an ordered search space and consistent boundary updates. Sorting first has a cost that belongs in the total analysis. Test missing values, duplicates and the smallest input rather than only a successful central match.'
  ],'Sorted array: [2, 4, 6, 8, 10]\nSearch 8: low=0, high=4, mid=2 -> value 6\nMove low=3; mid=3 -> value 8\nEach step preserves: if the target exists, it lies in [low, high].','The invariant explains correctness; the shrinking interval explains termination and logarithmic comparisons.',['Trace successful and unsuccessful binary searches.','Count comparisons as input size doubles.','Explain when sorting first is worth its cost.'],'Annotated trace, invariant and complexity comparison.','Can ordinary binary search be used on an unsorted array?','Not with its usual correctness guarantee; the ordering assumption is essential.'),
  unit('Choose a linked structure based on operations rather than its visual appearance.',[
   'A singly linked node stores a value and a next reference. Preserve the next reference before removing a node or the remaining chain may be lost. Handle head deletion separately and make ownership explicit in a native implementation.',
   'A doubly linked node also records its predecessor. Updates must preserve consistency in both directions. The extra links support backward traversal but require more storage and more careful insertion/deletion logic.',
   'A stack removes the most recently added item; a queue removes the oldest. Choose based on the required order. Define underflow behavior and test empty-to-nonempty transitions, which often reveal incorrect head/tail handling.',
   'A hash table maps keys to positions through a hash function and collision strategy. Expected constant-time lookup is not a guarantee for every input. Define key equality and test collisions rather than assuming every key maps to a unique bucket.'
  ],'Before: A -> B -> C -> null\nDelete B: save B.next as C; set A.next = C\nAfter: A -> C -> null\nDoubly linked version also sets C.prev = A\nCheck: deleting the only node leaves both endpoints empty.','A diagram is useful only when it shows which references change and which invariants remain true.',['Trace head, middle and tail deletion.','Compare stack and queue removal order.','Demonstrate two keys sharing a hash bucket.'],'Linked-structure traces and edge-case tests.','Why must a doubly linked deletion update both neighboring links?','Otherwise forward and backward traversal disagree and the structure becomes inconsistent.'),
  unit('Use tree structure to organize search and priority operations.',[
   'A binary tree allows at most two children per node. Define the empty tree and base case before writing recursion. Depth, height and traversal order describe different properties and should not be used interchangeably.',
   'A binary search tree maintains an ordering rule between each node and its subtrees. Insertion order affects shape. A skewed tree can reduce search to linear behavior, so logarithmic performance requires a balancing argument or assumption.',
   'Depth-first traversal explores a branch before returning; breadth-first traversal uses a queue to visit levels. Record visited nodes when cycles are possible in a more general graph. Choose order based on the question being answered.',
   'A heap maintains a priority relationship, not a fully sorted sequence. In a min-heap the minimum is available at the root. Removing it restores the heap invariant through a sift operation rather than sorting every element.'
  ],'Tree: root 8; left child 3; right child 10\nInorder: 3, 8, 10\nPreorder: 8, 3, 10\nLevel order: 8, 3, 10\nInsert 1,2,3,4 into an unbalanced BST to observe a skewed shape.','Traversal order depends on when a node is processed. A sorted inorder result relies on the BST ordering property.',['Draw three traversal orders.','Compare balanced and skewed search paths.','Trace a min-heap insertion and removal.'],'Tree explorer trace and invariant tests.','Is every element in a min-heap stored in sorted order?','No. The heap property orders parents against children, not every pair of elements.'),
  unit('Solve graph problems by matching the algorithm to the edge model.',[
   'Represent vertices and edges explicitly, including whether edges are directed or weighted. An adjacency list is efficient for many sparse graphs. Validate duplicate or missing vertex references and decide how self-loops are represented.',
   'BFS uses a queue and discovers vertices by edge distance in an unweighted graph. DFS explores depth first. Mark discovery consistently to avoid duplicate work in cycles, and represent disconnected vertices rather than silently ignoring them.',
   'BFS minimizes edge count only when that matches cost. Dijkstra’s algorithm requires nonnegative edge weights. State the assumptions before comparing results, and reconstruct a path from predecessor information instead of returning only a distance.',
   'Dynamic programming stores solutions to recurring subproblems. Define state, transition and base cases before implementation. The table dimensions come from the information needed for future decisions, not from the visual shape of the input.'
  ],'Edges: A-B, A-C, B-D, C-D, D-E\nBFS from A: distances A=0, B=1, C=1, D=2, E=3\nOne route to E: A-B-D-E\nMark D discovered when queued so C does not queue D again.','With adjacency lists, traversing the reachable graph takes O(V+E) time and O(V) auxiliary space in the usual bound.',['Run the graph simulation and record queue states.','Add a disconnected vertex and explain its result.','Change an edge weight and reconsider the shortest-path method.'],'Graph trace, path reconstruction and assumptions.','Does BFS always find the least-cost path in a weighted graph?','No. It minimizes edge count; unequal costs need an appropriate weighted-path algorithm.'),
  unit('Explain a correct solution before optimizing or presenting it.',[
   'Choose a pattern by recognizing an invariant or repeated subproblem, not by matching a keyword. Compare a simple baseline with the proposed approach. If the input assumptions differ, a familiar pattern may no longer be valid.',
   'Constraints determine feasible time and memory. Estimate the largest input and the size of stored state. An algorithm that is theoretically attractive may still exceed the available memory because its representation duplicates data.',
   'Timed practice should include reading, planning, implementation and validation. Reserve time for edge cases and explain a failed attempt. A fast answer that cannot be justified is weak evidence of understanding.',
   'Build a visualizer that distinguishes current state, discovered state and queued work. Provide step, reset and a written invariant. Test the algorithm independently from the animation so a convincing visual does not hide an incorrect computation.'
  ],'Solution record:\nInput constraints -> baseline -> selected invariant\nCorrectness argument -> time/space bound -> edge tests\nVisualizer states: initial, running, completed, unreachable\nKeep the algorithm output separate from the rendering code.','The capstone should make reasoning inspectable rather than merely animate nodes.',['Compare baseline and optimized approaches.','Solve a bounded problem set with explicit edge cases.','Deliver a tested step-by-step visualizer.'],'Algorithm visualizer, tests and complexity explanation.','Why test the algorithm separately from its animation?','Rendering can look plausible even when the underlying state transitions are wrong.')
 ],
 'full-stack':[
  unit('Build a semantic, responsive profile page before adding a framework.',[
   'HTML describes document meaning. Use headings in a coherent hierarchy and choose links for navigation and buttons for actions. A visual card does not automatically need a new semantic section; its role in the document determines the element.',
   'CSS separates presentation from structure. Use the box model, flexible layouts and bounded widths to accommodate content. Avoid fixed heights for text-heavy cards because translated or longer text can overflow even at the same viewport.',
   'JavaScript provides runtime behavior; TypeScript adds static checks during development. Types do not validate untrusted network data at runtime. Keep state changes explicit and verify input shapes at boundaries.',
   'Responsive design adapts content and controls, not just font size. Test keyboard focus, field labels, readable contrast and narrow layouts. A desktop screenshot cannot establish mobile usability or accessibility.'
  ],'<main>\n  <article aria-labelledby="profile-title">\n    <h1 id="profile-title">Learner profile</h1>\n    <p>My current learning project.</p>\n    <a href="./projects.html">View projects</a>\n  </article>\n</main>','The link navigates to another resource; it should not be replaced with a button merely to achieve a visual style.',['Create a semantic profile page.','Use a fluid card layout without a fixed text height.','Check keyboard focus and a narrow viewport.'],'Responsive page and accessibility checklist.','Does TypeScript validate arbitrary JSON after deployment?','No. Runtime boundary validation is still necessary.'),
  unit('Organize a frontend around components, explicit state and testable behavior.',[
   'A component has a focused responsibility and a clear input contract. Prefer composition over a large component that owns every interaction. Keep framework-specific rendering separate from business calculations where practical.',
   'State should represent the minimum information needed to render the interface. Avoid storing both a source value and a derived value that can drift apart. Forms need labels, validation feedback and predictable handling of unsaved changes.',
   'Data fetching introduces loading, failure, empty and stale states. Do not let an older response overwrite a newer request. Decide what should remain visible while work is pending and how a user can retry.',
   'Test components through observable behavior. A form test should enter data, submit and inspect feedback rather than only check implementation details. Include an error path and a keyboard interaction.'
  ],'UI state: {query, requestStatus, courses, error}\nDerived: visibleCourses = filter(courses, query)\nStates: idle -> loading -> success | error\nDo not store a second mutable copy of visibleCourses.','Derived state stays consistent when the query or source data changes.',['Split a course list into focused components.','Add loading and error feedback.','Test successful submission and invalid input.'],'Component interface, state diagram and UI tests.','Why avoid separately storing a value that can be derived from existing state?','Duplicated state can become inconsistent and requires unnecessary synchronization.'),
  unit('Create an API that enforces rules independently of its user interface.',[
   'An API contract defines resource identity, validation, response shape and errors. Use predictable status semantics and document pagination. A backend should not depend on the caller having used a particular screen.',
   'Authentication establishes identity; authorization evaluates whether that identity may perform an operation on a resource. Enforce ownership or organization scope on the server for every relevant request, including direct API calls.',
   'Validate types, ranges and required relationships at the boundary. Return useful but non-sensitive errors. Do not expose a database stack trace or internal credentials to explain an invalid request.',
   'Backend tests should exercise contracts, permissions and state transitions. Use isolated fixtures and verify that rejected requests have no unintended effect. A response code alone may not prove that the database remained unchanged.'
  ],'GET /projects/42\nCaller: learner-A; owner: learner-B\nExpected: access denied; no project payload\nPOST /projects: validate -> authorize -> write -> return ID\nTests include no session, wrong owner and malformed body.','A hidden UI button is not an authorization control. The service must reject an unauthorized direct request.',['Write an API contract.','Implement or diagram resource-level authorization.','Test rejection without a database mutation.'],'API specification and permission regression tests.','Is hiding an Edit button enough to prevent unauthorized edits?','No. The API must enforce the permission independently.'),
  unit('Connect data modeling, release practice and the final application.',[
   'SQL modeling defines keys, relationships and constraints that protect data integrity. Choose a grain for each table and use transactions for related changes that must succeed together. Indexes support access patterns but have write and storage costs.',
   'Document-oriented data models trade joins for embedded or referenced structures. Choose based on update and query patterns rather than fashion. Duplicated values need an explicit consistency strategy.',
   'A container packages an application environment; CI verifies and builds changes. Keep secrets outside images and test the production artifact. Deployments need health checks and rollback planning, including database compatibility.',
   'The capstone integrates a responsive frontend, validated API, data model and tests. Demonstrate failures and permissions as well as the happy path. Include setup instructions and identify simulated integrations honestly.'
  ],'Release checklist:\nSchema changes reviewed -> tests -> build -> smoke test\nVerify: create, read, update, reject unauthorized request\nRecord: environment variables required, migration order, rollback limits\nNever commit a real secret in a starter project.','A reproducible release is part of the project evidence, not an optional final screenshot.',['Model the project’s records and relationships.','Choose one SQL or document access pattern and justify it.','Deliver the integrated application with tests and setup instructions.'],'Full-stack capstone and deployment runbook.','Can rolling back code always reverse a destructive schema change?','No. Data and schema compatibility need a separate migration and recovery plan.')
 ],
 'data-analytics':[
  unit('Turn a stakeholder question into a metric that can be reproduced.',[
   'Define the decision before selecting a chart. A KPI needs a numerator, denominator, time window and eligible population. Counts and rates answer different questions; show both when population sizes differ.',
   'A spreadsheet analysis should preserve raw data and separate calculations from presentation. Use explicit ranges and inspect missing or inconsistent types. A formatted number is not proof that the underlying formula uses the right records.',
   'Data quality includes completeness, validity, uniqueness and consistency. Record exclusions and their effect on the result. Do not silently turn missing values into zeros because that changes the meaning of the analysis.',
   'Analytical thinking distinguishes observation, interpretation and recommendation. Seek an alternative explanation for a pattern. A descriptive comparison does not establish causation, especially when groups differ in timing or selection.'
  ],'Cohort A: 80 completions / 100 eligible = 80%\nCohort B: 60 completions / 60 eligible = 100%\nA has more completions; B has the higher completion rate\nCheck equal follow-up periods before comparing.','A missing denominator can reverse the interpretation without changing any completion count.',['Write a metric definition.','Reconcile a spreadsheet summary with raw records.','Explain one alternative interpretation of the result.'],'Metric dictionary and reconciled analysis.','Why show the denominator with a percentage?','It reveals the eligible population and helps users interpret sample size and comparability.'),
  unit('Build an analysis dataset with a known row grain.',[
   'Start a SQL query by identifying what one output row represents. Filter deliberately and distinguish WHERE from conditions applied after aggregation. A correct syntax can still answer the wrong business question.',
   'Joins can multiply rows when keys are not unique. Inspect cardinality before aggregating. Counting joined rows is not the same as counting distinct learners when one learner has several events.',
   'A cleaning workflow should be repeatable and preserve an audit trail. Standardize types and labels, document imputation and inspect duplicates. Keep a raw copy so a disputed transformation can be reviewed.',
   'A reusable dataset includes its grain, keys, definitions and refresh assumptions. Avoid embedding a different definition of the same metric in every dashboard. Validate totals against a trusted source before publishing.'
  ],'SELECT cohort_id, COUNT(DISTINCT learner_id) AS learners\nFROM eligible_enrollments\nGROUP BY cohort_id;\nBefore joining lesson_events, inspect events per learner\nAn event-level join changes the row grain.','Distinct counting can address one duplication problem, but the eligibility definition still needs to be correct.',['State the grain before writing a query.','Demonstrate a one-to-many join that inflates counts.','Create a documented, reusable cohort dataset.'],'SQL queries, cardinality checks and dataset contract.','Why can a join inflate an enrollment count?','A matching one-to-many table can create several joined rows for one enrollment.'),
  unit('Explore a dataset without confusing patterns with proof.',[
   'A Pandas workflow should make selection, transformation and aggregation readable. Inspect dtypes and nulls before calculating. Avoid accidental chained updates or hidden mutation by working with explicit intermediate results.',
   'Exploratory analysis checks distributions, outliers and relationships before a final narrative. Investigate unusual values rather than deleting them automatically. A surprising observation may be a data error or a real part of the population.',
   'Choose summary statistics that fit the distribution and question. A mean can be sensitive to extreme values; a median answers a different question. Show variability and sample size instead of presenting one number as complete evidence.',
   'Experiment interpretation needs a design, outcome definition and appropriate uncertainty assessment. Distinguish randomized experiments from observational comparisons. Repeatedly searching for a favorable result can make an apparently convincing finding unreliable.'
  ],'Study values: [2, 3, 3, 4, 38]\nMean = 10; median = 3\nInvestigate 38: measurement error or a real long-duration case?\nReport the reason for any exclusion rather than deleting it silently.','Both summaries are mathematically valid, but they describe different aspects of the data.',['Inspect types and missing values.','Compare mean, median and spread.','Write an experiment interpretation with limitations.'],'Notebook, exploratory plots and interpretation memo.','Should every outlier be removed?','No. Investigate its origin and relevance; valid extreme observations may be important.'),
  unit('Communicate a decision with readable visuals and honest caveats.',[
   'Choose visual encodings that fit the comparison. Use position and length for precise comparisons and label units. Avoid decorative effects that distort scale or imply precision the data does not support.',
   'A dashboard should support a small set of user decisions. Group related metrics, expose filters and show the reporting period. Include empty and missing-data states so an unavailable result is not mistaken for zero.',
   'A stakeholder narrative connects the question, method, finding, limitations and proposed action. Distinguish the evidence from the recommendation. A clear caveat strengthens the decision when it explains what could change the conclusion.',
   'The capstone combines a reproducible dataset, analysis, dashboard and recommendation. Make metric definitions and exclusions available. A screenshot alone does not demonstrate that another analyst can reproduce the result.'
  ],'Narrative: question -> source and window -> finding -> limitation -> action\nDashboard label: Completion rate, eligible cohort, reporting date\nAnnotation: newer cohort has incomplete follow-up\nRecommendation: compare again after the same observation window.','The caveat changes how the result should be used, so it belongs near the chart rather than hidden in a footer.',['Create a chart with units and sample size.','Add explicit filter and missing-data states.','Present a reproducible capstone recommendation.'],'Dashboard, source queries and stakeholder memo.','Where should a limitation that changes a chart’s interpretation appear?','Close to the relevant finding, where the decision-maker can see it.')
 ]
});
Object.assign(moduleContent,{
 'cybersecurity':[
  unit('Review an isolated learning application using synthetic records and explicit authorization to test.',[
   'Confidentiality, integrity and availability describe different security needs. Identify the asset and the consequence of a failure before selecting a control. A useful review explains which risk a control reduces and which risk remains.',
   'An attack surface includes inputs, dependencies, interfaces and administrative access. Model plausible capabilities rather than assuming every threat actor has unlimited access. Stay within the authorized lab and do not test unrelated systems.',
   'A threat model connects actors, assets, trust boundaries and abuse cases. Draw how data moves and where a less-trusted input crosses into a privileged operation. Prioritize based on impact and feasibility rather than the visual size of a diagram.',
   'Preventive, detective and recovery controls serve different purposes. Combine them with clear ownership and tests. A control that exists in a policy document but is not enforced in the application should not be counted as effective protection.'
  ],'Asset: private project notes\nBoundary: browser -> API -> database\nAbuse case: learner requests another learner’s project\nControl: server-side resource authorization\nEvidence: authorized request succeeds; cross-user request is denied.','The exercise tests the boundary with synthetic accounts, not real users or third-party services.',['Draw the lab’s trust boundaries.','Write three scoped abuse cases.','Map each case to a control and verification method.'],'Threat model and authorized test plan.','Is the existence of a written policy enough to establish that a control works?','No. Enforcement and verification evidence are needed.'),
  unit('Keep identity, permission and network exposure separate in the design.',[
   'Authentication establishes who is calling; authorization decides what that caller may do. Check the resource as well as the operation. A valid login does not justify access to every record.',
   'Least privilege limits permissions to the task and duration needed. Review service accounts as well as people. Remove unused access and test denial paths; adding a role label without enforcement does not create a boundary.',
   'Network controls restrict reachability but do not replace application authorization. Identify public and private interfaces, allowed flows and administrative paths. Use a local diagram or simulator for the exercise instead of scanning an external network.',
   'Cloud security includes identity, configuration, secrets, data protection and operational monitoring. Responsibility is shared between the provider and the customer. A managed service does not automatically validate application permissions or protect a leaked credential.'
  ],'Access matrix:\nOwner: read/update own project\nOther learner: no access\nReviewer: read only when explicitly assigned\nAnonymous: no private data\nTest each row against the same project fixture.','Testing the same resource under different identities reveals authorization errors that a single successful login cannot.',['Build a least-privilege matrix.','Draw permitted network flows.','Review a synthetic cloud configuration for unnecessary access.'],'Access matrix and boundary test evidence.','Does placing an API on a private network remove the need for authorization?','No. Reachability and permission are different controls.'),
  unit('Validate application boundaries throughout development.',[
   'Use risk categories as prompts for investigation, not a complete security checklist. Identify the actual input, control and consequence in the lab. A category name alone does not establish that a specific vulnerability exists.',
   'Validate requests and enforce object-level permission before returning private data. A complex identifier is not authorization. Test an owner, another user and no session with synthetic records.',
   'Keep secrets out of source code and browser bundles. Limit who can read sensitive data and avoid unnecessary copies in logs. Encryption does not make unrestricted access acceptable; key handling and authorization still matter.',
   'Build security review into design, implementation, testing and release. Track findings with owners and retest evidence. A fix should include a regression case so the same failure is less likely to return.'
  ],'GET /projects/42\nSession: learner-A; record owner: learner-B\nRequired outcome: deny unless explicit sharing grants access\nRegression cases: owner allowed; other user denied; no session denied\nLog decision metadata without returning the private record.','Randomizing the project ID would not repair the missing permission check.',['Review a lab API boundary.','Create owner/non-owner/no-session tests.','Document a repair and its regression evidence.'],'Application review and remediation report.','Does replacing sequential IDs with UUIDs fix an authorization failure?','No. Identifier complexity does not establish permission.'),
  unit('Make security events useful for investigation and recovery.',[
   'A log should identify the event, time, actor or service and decision without exposing unnecessary sensitive payloads. Use consistent identifiers to connect related events. Decide retention and access controls before collecting large volumes.',
   'A detection rule maps observable behavior to a review signal. Define what the rule can and cannot detect, and test normal behavior as well as the suspicious case. Too many false alarms can make a technically active control operationally ineffective.',
   'An incident process covers triage, containment, investigation, recovery and learning. Preserve evidence and assign communication responsibilities. In the course, use a tabletop scenario; do not take disruptive action on a live system.',
   'The capstone presents scope, assets, findings, controls, residual risk and retest evidence. Separate confirmed observations from hypotheses. A student review is not a compliance certification or a guarantee that no vulnerabilities remain.'
  ],'Tabletop: repeated denied requests for private records\nTriage: confirm timestamps, scope and identity\nContainment proposal: review access and session state\nEvidence: request IDs and decisions, not full private records\nRecovery: verify control; document lessons and follow-up owner.','An alert becomes useful when it leads to an accountable investigation, not merely another dashboard count.',['Define a minimal security event schema.','Test a detection rule with positive and negative fixtures.','Run a tabletop and present the final review.'],'Security event plan, tabletop record and capstone report.','Does a clean test run prove that no vulnerabilities remain?','No. It establishes only the tested behavior within the stated scope and assumptions.')
 ],
 'applied-ml-engineering':[
  unit('Prepare a reproducible dataset and a defensible prediction question.',[
   'Use Python transformations with explicit input and output shapes. Preserve a raw fixture and set reproducibility controls where appropriate. A notebook cell that depends on hidden execution order is difficult to review or rerun.',
   'Statistics helps describe variation and uncertainty. Distinguish a population from a sample and a correlation from a causal explanation. The evaluation question determines which summary or estimate is useful.',
   'Define the prediction time before selecting features. Exclude information that would not exist at that time. Split data before fitting learned preprocessing so held-out examples do not influence training transformations.',
   'SQL extracts records at a defined grain; visualization reveals distributions and quality problems. Inspect join cardinality and missing values before training. A clean-looking chart does not establish that the dataset is free of leakage.'
  ],'Prediction: churn in the next month\nAllowed feature: activity before the cutoff\nExcluded feature: account closure reason populated afterward\nSplit: earlier periods for training; later held-out period for evaluation\nFit preprocessing only on training data.','A post-outcome field can make a model appear accurate by revealing information unavailable at prediction time.',['Define a prediction cutoff.','Create a feature-availability table.','Build a reproducible cleaning and split workflow.'],'Dataset contract and leakage review.','Why exclude a field created after the outcome?','It would not be available when making the real prediction and can leak the target.'),
  unit('Compare simple models using an evaluation design that reflects deployment.',[
   'Regression predicts a quantity; classification predicts a category or class probability. Establish a simple baseline before fitting a complex model. The business decision determines which errors matter and how predictions will be used.',
   'Features should encode information available at inference time. Fit transformations using training data and apply the learned transformation to held-out data. A feature that identifies the outcome indirectly can still leak the target.',
   'Clustering groups examples according to a representation and similarity choice. It does not automatically discover useful business segments. Inspect stability and interpretability, and avoid assigning meaning to cluster numbers alone.',
   'Select metrics that match class balance and error costs. Keep model selection separate from final testing. Choose a decision threshold on validation evidence and report limitations instead of presenting one aggregate score as universal quality.'
  ],'Compare: majority baseline vs classifier\nReport: confusion matrix, precision, recall and threshold\nSelect settings on validation data\nUse the final held-out set once for the final estimate\nInspect subgroup and time-period limitations.','A credible lower score is more useful than a high score produced by repeated adaptation to the test set.',['Fit or outline a baseline comparison.','Explain a feature transformation without leakage.','Choose metrics and a threshold for a stated decision.'],'Model comparison and evaluation memo.','Why not repeatedly choose settings on the final test set?','The choices adapt to that set and weaken its independence as an evaluation.'),
  unit('Understand how neural networks learn and where training can fail.',[
   'A neural network composes parameterized transformations. Specify input shape, output target and loss before choosing layers. More parameters do not compensate for an unclear target or invalid evaluation split.',
   'Optimization updates parameters to reduce training loss. Learning rate, batching and initialization influence behavior. Track validation performance as well as training loss, and investigate divergence rather than assuming more training will repair it.',
   'Convolutional layers share weights across spatial positions. They are useful for image structure but still require appropriate data, preprocessing and evaluation. Check how resizing or normalization changes the information the model receives.',
   'Sequence models operate on ordered representations; attention relates positions through learned scores. Explain token or time-step shapes before assembling a transformer. An educational model illustrates mechanisms but is not equivalent to a production-scale language model.'
  ],'Training trace:\nEpoch 1: training loss decreases; validation improves\nLater: training loss decreases; validation worsens\nInvestigate overfitting, split quality and stopping policy\nPreserve the selected checkpoint and its configuration.','Training improvement alone is not evidence of better generalization.',['Draw tensor shapes through a small network.','Compare two learning rates on a controlled fixture.','Explain a validation-based stopping decision.'],'Training notebook, shape diagram and learning curves.','What can declining training loss with worsening validation loss indicate?','Potential overfitting or a distribution/evaluation issue requiring investigation.'),
  unit('Evaluate perception tasks according to their actual outputs.',[
   'Classification labels an image; detection locates and labels objects. Choose labels and metrics that match the task. A classifier cannot be evaluated as if it produced reliable object boundaries.',
   'Segmentation labels regions, tracking associates observations across time and OCR extracts text. Each introduces different failure modes. Inspect small objects, occlusion, reading order and missing text rather than relying only on an average metric.',
   'NLP workflows require a defined text unit, label policy and evaluation set. Tokenization and truncation can remove useful information. Keep sensitive text governed and inspect errors by input type.',
   'Combining voice, image and text does not remove uncertainty from the inputs. Preserve source provenance and detect missing modalities. Define a fallback when one channel fails instead of silently inventing a complete record.'
  ],'Document task: extract invoice fields from synthetic scans\nEvaluate: field correctness, missing fields and page references\nCases: rotated text, low contrast, multiple totals, missing page\nRoute uncertain fields to review; do not fabricate values.','A useful extraction workflow represents uncertainty and missing evidence explicitly.',['Specify the output for two perception tasks.','Create a small edge-case evaluation set.','Design a missing-modality fallback.'],'Perception evaluation and error analysis.','Should an OCR failure be replaced with a plausible-looking value?','No. Preserve missing or uncertain status and request review.'),
  unit('Adapt a model only after defining the behavior and evaluation gap.',[
   'Training data needs provenance, consistent formatting and an explicit label policy. Separate development and evaluation examples. Review duplication and permissions before using a dataset, not after an apparently successful training run.',
   'Supervised fine-tuning adjusts a pretrained model using target examples. It can improve a bounded behavior but does not guarantee current factual knowledge or safe action execution. Compare against a prompt or retrieval baseline.',
   'Parameter-efficient adaptation updates a subset or additional low-rank parameters. Quantized adaptation can reduce memory requirements, but setup and quality trade-offs must be measured. Do not assume the smallest training footprint gives the best deployed result.',
   'Quantization reduces representation precision; distillation trains a smaller model to approximate useful behavior. Evaluate quality, latency and resource use on the real task. Compression can change failure patterns even when aggregate performance appears similar.'
  ],'Adaptation experiment:\nBaseline -> fixed evaluation set -> one controlled adaptation\nCompare task quality, unsupported outputs, latency and memory\nKeep dataset version, configuration and checkpoint ID\nReject improvement claims that use a changed test set.','A controlled comparison keeps the task and evaluation stable while changing the model treatment.',['Write a dataset card.','Compare adaptation with a non-training baseline.','Document compression trade-offs and error changes.'],'Adaptation experiment and model card.','Does fine-tuning remove the need for external evidence and authorization?','No. Those are separate system responsibilities.'),
  unit('Connect an educational transformer to a reproducible serving workflow.',[
   'A tokenizer maps input text to token identifiers; embeddings map identifiers to learned vectors. Record vocabulary and preprocessing versions with the model. A mismatch between training and serving tokenization can invalidate otherwise correct weights.',
   'A training loop performs forward computation, loss evaluation, gradient computation and parameter updates. Handle padding and masks consistently. Use a small educational dataset to inspect the mechanics rather than imply production-language capability.',
   'Serving needs a versioned input contract, resource limits and health checks. Load the matching preprocessing artifacts and model. Define behavior for invalid or oversized input before exposing an endpoint.',
   'Monitoring tracks data quality, latency and task outcomes after deployment. Drift is a signal to investigate, not automatic proof that retraining will help. The capstone should include rollback and evaluation of a proposed replacement.'
  ],'Artifact bundle: tokenizer + model + preprocessing + config + evaluation\nEndpoint: validate input -> transform -> predict -> return version\nMonitor: invalid inputs, latency, distribution changes and reviewed errors\nReplacement: evaluate before promotion; keep rollback artifact.','A model file alone is not a reproducible prediction service. The surrounding artifacts define how inputs are interpreted.',['Trace a tiny training loop.','Define a versioned serving contract.','Present a capstone with monitoring and rollback plans.'],'Educational model, serving prototype and MLOps report.','Does detected drift always mean the model must be retrained immediately?','No. Investigate the cause and measured task impact before deciding on a remedy.')
 ]
});
